#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# map_indexed

#% text
# `map_indexed(f, xs)` gives `[f(x1, 1), f(x2, 2), ...]`: `f` applied to each element of `xs` and its position.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `f` takes two arguments, the element first and the position second.

#% item
# Positions start at 1.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
map_indexed(f, [a, b, c])
#% output
#= [f(a, 1), f(b, 2), f(c, 3)]

#% cell
map_indexed((x, i) => x*i, [10, 20, 30])
#% output
#= [10, 40, 90]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Numbered labels:

#% cell
map_indexed((s, i) => string_join([to_string(i), ". ", s]), ["mass", "charge", "spin"])
#% output
#= ["1. mass", "2. charge", "3. spin"]

#% text
# A polynomial from its coefficients, lowest power first:

#% cell
total(map_indexed((c, i) => c*x^(i - 1), [1, -2, 0, 4]))
#% output
#= 1 + -2*x + 4*x^3

#% text
# The pairs of a list's values with their positions, for `list_plot`:

#% cell
map_indexed((y, i) => [i, y], [0.5, 0.8, 0.6])
#% output
#= [[1, 0.5], [2, 0.8], [3, 0.6]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The same with `map_thread` over the list and its positions:

#% cell
xs = [a, b, c]
map_indexed(f, xs) === map_thread(f, [xs, range(length(xs))])
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `map`, `map_thread`, `array`, `table`
